## Proof

**Claim:** For $n \geq 3$, the intersection $N(P) \cap N(Q)$ is always nonempty.

We use the **Projective Dimension Theorem**: *If $X, Y \subseteq \mathbb{CP}^m$ are nonempty closed subvarieties of dimensions $r$ and $s$, then every irreducible component of $X \cap Y$ has dimension $\geq r + s - m$. In particular, if $r + s \geq m$, then $X \cap Y \neq \emptyset$.*

---

**Case 1: $P \neq 0$ and $Q \neq 0$.**

Each of $N(P)$, $N(Q)$ is a hypersurface of degree $d$ in $\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}$, hence a closed subvariety of pure dimension $n - 2$.

Apply the Projective Dimension Theorem with $m = n-1$, $r = s = n-2$:

$$r + s - m = (n-2)+(n-2)-(n-1) = n - 3 \geq 0 \quad (\text{since } n \geq 3).$$

Since $r + s \geq m$, we conclude $N(P) \cap N(Q) \neq \emptyset$. $\checkmark$

---

**Case 2: At least one of $P, Q$ is the zero polynomial.**

Without loss, say $P = 0$. Then $N(P) = \mathbb{CP}^{n-1}$, so $N(P) \cap N(Q) = N(Q)$.

- If $Q = 0$ as well, then $N(Q) = \mathbb{CP}^{n-1} \neq \emptyset$. $\checkmark$
- If $Q \neq 0$, then $N(Q)$ is a hypersurface in $\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}$ with $n-1 \geq 2$. To see this is nonempty: restrict $Q$ to any projective line $\ell \cong \mathbb{CP}^1$ in $\mathbb{CP}^{n-1}$ (such a line exists since $n-1 \geq 1$). The restriction is a nonzero homogeneous polynomial of degree $d \geq 1$ in two variables, which factors completely over $\mathbb{C}$ into $d$ linear factors and thus has at least one zero in $\ell \subseteq \mathbb{CP}^{n-1}$. Hence $N(Q) \neq \emptyset$. $\checkmark$

---

**Conclusion:** In every case, for $n \geq 3$, the intersection $N(P) \cap N(Q)$ contains at least one point. $\blacksquare$

